  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Given name first, then the family name. English, Scottish and Irish family names run through English Canada; Quebec has its own small set of very common French names (Tremblay, Gagnon, Roy, Côté) and hyphenated given names among older people (Jean-François, Marie-Ève). In Quebec women keep their own family name after marriage. Sikh Punjabis carry Singh (men) or Kaur (women) as a middle name before the family name (Harpreet Singh Gill, Simran Kaur Sandhu), and some use Singh or Kaur as the family name. Chinese Canadians from Hong Kong often have an English first name and a two-part Cantonese name (Kevin Ka Ho Chan); newer arrivals from mainland China use pinyin spellings (Zhang, Liu).",
